% ============================================================================
% adverb-type — four named adverb types and what each actually describes
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Adverbs modify a verb, adjective, or another adverb, and grammar
% references sort them into overlapping categories by WHAT question they
% answer: an adverb of manner answers "how", an adverb of place answers
% "where", an adverb of frequency answers "how often", and an adverb of
% duration answers "how long". Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? adverb_type(manner, $D)
%       % describes_how_an_action_is_performed
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `adverb_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on an adverb type that is not one of these four. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the adverb type
% it names:
%
%     ? adverb_type($T, describes_how_long_an_action_lasts)
%       % duration
%
% The four adverb types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type          description                                quote (verbatim)
%     ------------  -----------------------------------------  ------------------------------------------------------
%     manner         describes_how_an_action_is_performed        "Adverbs of manner describe how an action is performed"
%     place          describes_where_an_action_happens            "Adverbs of place describe where an action happens"
%     frequency      describes_how_often_an_action_occurs         "Adverbs of frequency describe how often an action occurs"
%     duration       describes_how_long_an_action_lasts           "Adverbs of duration describe how long an action lasts"
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "What Is an Adverb?
% Definition and Examples" article's "Types of adverbs" table. WebFetch-
% verified twice — the second pass pulled every row of that table,
% confirming manner/place/frequency/duration are each stated as their own
% clean, single-fact sentence ("Adverbs of X describe/describes <one
% thing>"), while a fifth row, adverbs of TIME, is deliberately excluded
% (see below). `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a reputable, widely-cited
% grammar-content company, the same tier this stdlib already reserves for
% other non-.gov language sources and the same source family already
% cited by `verb-type.adj`/`sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`/
% `noun-type.adj`/`preposition-type.adj`. Picked using the mandatory
% full-tree-grep-before-scoping discipline — `grep -rilE
% "\badverb_type\b|\badverbs_of_manner\b"
% code/specs/data/adj-facts-stdlib/` found ZERO hits, confirming a
% completely fresh topic before this file was written; closes out
% adverbs as the last major part-of-speech family this stdlib had not yet
% named on its own (noun/pronoun/verb/preposition/conjunction/determiner
% already shipped as sibling `*-type.adj` tables).
%
% Honest abstention on "time": the SAME table on the same page also names
% adverbs of time, but its own defining sentence — "Adverbs of time
% describe when, how long, or how often something happens" — bundles
% THREE distinct facts (when, how long, how often) into one sentence
% rather than stating a single clean fact the way manner/place/frequency/
% duration each do (and duration/frequency individually already cover two
% of those three questions on their own). This is the same "reject
% bundled facts" discipline applied across recent slices (fossil-
% preservation-type, lunar-eclipse-type, comma-rule, sun-layer) — prefer
% a smaller honestly-scoped table over one padded with a compound
% sentence. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table adverb_type {
    columns type, description

    row (manner, describes_how_an_action_is_performed)
    row (place, describes_where_an_action_happens)
    row (frequency, describes_how_often_an_action_occurs)
    row (duration, describes_how_long_an_action_lasts)

    source "Adverbs of manner describe how an action is performed"
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/adverb/"
    trust consensus
}
